data.reasoningText:
Perfect! Now I see the issue. According to the code:

```typescript
export const isCatalogedApiUnit = (unit: TranslatableUnit): boolean => unit.kind === "prose";
```

This means only units with `kind === "prose"` are included in the catalog (PO files). Units with `kind === "glossary"` are NOT cataloged.

Now let me look at how the POT is built in build-api-locales.ts:

```typescript
const refreshApiPot = (): void => {
  const units = globSync("**/*.md", { cwd: enApiDir })
    .sort()
    .flatMap((file) =>
      collectUnits(segmentMarkdown(readFileSync(join(enApiDir, file), "utf8")))
        // Glossary units are deterministically substituted and never written to the PO catalog
        // (see segment-markdown.ts) — including them here would make 100% coverage unreachable.
        .filter(isCatalogedApiUnit)
        .map((unit) => ({
          msgid: unit.text,
          msgctxt: `docs.api:${unit.kind}`,
          reference: relative(enApiDir, join(enApiDir, file)),
          translate: "always" as const,
        })),
    );
  writeCatalog(join(repoRoot, "l10n", "docs.api.pot"), serializePot(units, ["no-c-format"]));
  refreshCoverageReports(join(repoRoot, "i18n.config.json"));
};
```

So the POT is being built correctly with `docs.api:prose` msgctxt for prose units.

Now let me look at translate-api-po.ts and check-locale-drift.ts to see the issue:

In translate-api-po.ts:
```typescript
const entries = parsePo(readFileSync(path, "utf8"));
const missing = entries.filter((entry) => !entry.obsolete && entry.msgstr === "");
```

Then it builds a lookup map keyed on msgctxt+msgid combined with a null separator:

```typescript
const byId = new Map(missing.map((entry) => [`${entry.msgctxt ?? ""}\0${entry.msgid}`, entry]));
```

Checking check-locale-drift.ts next for how API drift detection handles the same locale/docs.api.po path.

}
}
return missing;
}

Cross-checking with build-api-locales.ts: msgctxt is built as `docs.api:${unit.kind}`, and since isCatalogedApiUnit only passes prose units, that resolves to `docs.api:prose`. So the key format lines up correctly—`docs.api:prose\0{msgid}` matches what check-locale-drift.ts is testing against.

Now investigating "filled 0; remaining 0"—in translate-api-po.ts, missing is filtered from non-obsolete empty msgstr entries. The log statement only fires inside the `if (missing.length > 0)` block, meaning if there's nothing missing, that log never prints at all.

Wait, let me trace through again. Actually the console.log sits outside the if block, so it always executes regardless. It logs the initial missing count and then recomputes remaining afterward. So "filled 0; remaining 0" means missing.length was 0 to begin with—no empty

msgstr entries existed when the script ran, and none remained. That likely means the PO file has no entries at all, or everything was already translated.

For "docs.api missing units", the PO file probably lacks entries that should exist based on generated API docs—maybe the POT generation is broken, the PO was never merged, or msgctxt keys don't align between build-api-locales and check-locale-drift. I should check the tests to clarify the expected pipeline.

Now I see the test setup! Looking at line:

```typescript
fixtures.apiPo = apiProseSources
  .map((source) => `msgctxt "docs.api:prose"\nmsgid "${source}"\nmsgstr "translated"\n`)
  .join("\n");
```

So the PO file is expected to have entries with `msgctxt "docs.api:prose"` and msgid as the source text.

But wait, let me look at what's being checked in the test. In the test:

```typescript
test("PO-backed drift still reports missing prose", async () => {
    const { apiDrift } = await import("./check-locale-drift.ts");
    fixtures.apiFiles = ["page.md"];
    fixtures.apiPo = `msgctxt "docs.api:prose"\nmsgid "${apiProseSources[0]}"\nmsgstr "translated"\n`;
    const missing = apiDrift("hu");
    expect(missing).toHaveLength(3);
    expect(missing.every((item) => item.kind === "prose")).toBe(true);
  });
```

So if there are 4 prose blocks in API_MD and only 1 is translated, it should report 3 missing.

Now looking back at check-locale-drift.ts's apiDrift function:

```typescript
export const apiDrift = (locale: string): Missing[] => {
  const poPath = join(l10nDir, locale, "docs.api.po");
  if (existsSync(poPath)) {
    const translated = new Set(
      parsePo(readFileSync(poPath, "utf8"))
        .filter((entry) => entry.msgstr !== "")
        .map((entry) => `${entry.msgctxt ?? ""}\0${entry.msgid}`),
    );
    const missing: Missing[] = [];
    for (const file of walkMarkdown(apiDir)) {
      for (const unit of collectUnits(segmentMarkdown(readFileSync(file, "utf8")))) {
        if (!isCatalogedApiUnit(unit)) continue;
        if (!translated.has(`docs.api:${unit.kind}\0${unit.text}`)) {
          missing.push({
            file: relative(docsRoot, file),
            kind: unit.kind,
            sample: preview(unit.text),
          });
        }
      }
    }
    return missing;
  }
  // ... cache-based fallback
};
```

The key format is `docs.api:${unit.kind}\0${unit.text}` — matching against msgctxt/msgid pairs from the PO. So it iterates every unit in every API markdown file, keeps only cataloged ones, and flags any whose key isn't in the translated set.

Given `filled 0; remaining 0` from translate-api-po.ts, I suspect the PO file is either missing or empty — causing check-locale-drift to either fall back to a cache-based check or find zero matches against an empty translated set. Need to trace build-api-locales.ts's buildLocale flow (adapter setup, translation memory, cloning, translating markdown/sidebars) to see where the disconnect happens.

Actually, build-api-locales.ts only builds translated pages — it doesn't touch the PO catalogs. So translate-api-po.ts must source its data elsewhere: it reads `l10n/{locale}/docs.api.po` directly via `parsePo`, filtering for non-obsolete empty msgstr entries. The real question is where this PO file is supposed to originate from.

Tracing the workflow, build-api-locales.ts calls `refreshApiPot()` first, which rebuilds `l10n/docs.api.pot` from the EN API tree just generated — keeping the coverage denominator in sync so stale POT keys don't get compared against fresh PO entries.

readFileSync(join(enApiDir, file), "utf8"))) then filters for catalogedApiUnit, since glossary units are deterministically substituted and never written to the PO catalog — including them would make 100% coverage unreachable. It maps each remaining unit to msgid/msgctxt/reference/translate fields, writes the catalog, then calls refreshCoverageReports.

Now checking translate-api-po.ts, which reads a PO file expected to exist from merging this POT. Looking at runTranslateContent in i18n-engine/src/pipeline.ts — it re-extracts first, since msgmerge can only propagate...

it can only propagate units the POT already knows about. It calls runExtractContent to get the potPath, then resolves the poPath from the target catalog pattern. For file-segmented spaces, it uses the Markdown-aware fill function instead of the regular one. Then it calls mergeAndCount with potPath, poPath, and the fill callback, and refreshes coverage reports afterward.

So runTranslateContent re-extracts, merges, fills, and returns. But build-api-locales.ts skips this entire pipeline — it translates markdown pages inline using TranslationMemory and adapters directly, without touching the PO files at all. That means translate-api-po.ts later expects an up-to-date l10n/{locale}/docs.api.po but finds it stale, missing, or out of sync.

The likely culprit is a missing coordination step — nothing ties build-api-locales.ts's POT generation to translate-api-po.ts's PO filling. I should check for an orchestrating script that calls both in sequence, or search how the docs:api workflow is wired together.

